nse to cancer  after that we also created our breast  cancer 360 panel  owing to our deep relationships in the  breast cancer space  through development of prosigna  the breast cancer 360 panel again  focuses on all of the biology  but primarily on the tumor and  specifically breast tumors  and then most recently we've created the  encounter tumor signaling 360 panel  to focus on the biology of the tumor but  do so in a way that's more generic for  all solid tumor  studies this this is the panel that i'll  be talking about  for the remainder of our our talk here  so the reason why we believe this tool  to be worthwhile to develop  is that targeted therapies continue to  be the focus for drug development  you can think about parp inhibitors braf  or mech inhibitors idh1 therapies and so  on  and we believe that it was time to  create a tool specifically  to be a companion for these sorts of  studies  and as an example of that back in 2015  when we created our pan cancer pathways  panel we started with some of the most  canonical pathways  in cancer biology this list should come  as no surprise to many of you and  includes  map kinase pi3 kinase the cell cycle  and so on things that are important and  have been known to be important in  cancer  for a great number of years however in  2018  as many of you may remember the cancer  genome atlas consortium  uh came out with a series of papers that  highlighted some of the best results  from their studies over the years and  from the cancer  genome atlas data set  one such paper focused on oncogenic  signaling pathways  in cancer and as you can see they  published a number of these  pathways and many of them were not even  included in that pain cancer pathways  panel owing to the fact that oncology as  a field continues to move  and the number of pathways and diversity  of pathways we consider for targeted  therapies  continues to develop and evolve  and so for developing this panel we  started with our best-in-class panel  development framework  process this process starts with  foundational scientific frameworks at  least where they exist  fortunately in oncology we can rely on  the  hanahan and weinberg hallmarks of cancer  which as you know is kind of a  foundational and complete framework  for thinking about questions in oncology  we can also then integrate  state-of-the-art knowledge  up to the date uh publications and other  resources  in the in the scientific literature that  help us make sure that all of the  content is up to date  and informative and then we engage our  research community resources  things like public databases high  throughput data  key opinion leaders our customers  working groups  and so on and then finally when we're  done putting together our panels we  always make sure that they integrate  well with our encounter advanced  analysis software  this software uses the pathways that we  embed into the product  to give you an understanding of pathway  regulation  and give you scores that help you  understand how the biology is moving  in your data set and then we also have a  feature called our cell type profiling  our cell type profiling is embedded in  the tumor signaling 360 panel  and many of our other panels and gives  you a relative abundance of various  immune cell types  in your your sample this is particularly  powerful especially in oncology  where such a large fraction of samples  are in ffpe  and thus cannot be subjected to cell  type sorting  or other methods of cell type abundance  scoring  so for this particular panel we started  again with the literature search  making sure we covered all hallmarks of  cancer and cancer-relevant pathways  that gave us 80 different unique sources  for genes that included databases  literature and published signatures  overall we considered over 5000 genes  and these were annotated with respect to  pathways  and then we used our internal  development software  to assess the most informative genes in  each of those pathways  within the context of tcga with  consideration in some cases for tumor  type specific pathways  in a fashion i'll explain a bit in a few  slides  and then finally we always take feedback  from our key opinion leaders to make  sure that we've  included all the content and genes that  would be relevant  for general studies so in the end we  have a comprehensive  780 gene list covering tumor signaling  the immune response and the tumor  microenvironment  and so in in owing to our goal to  incorporate biology relevant to targeted  therapies  we use the particular resource to try to  cover a number of the target genes from  fda approved and developing targeted  therapies  these include targets like stat 3  her 3 and so on just to try to make sure  again that this tool is the most  up-to-date  and relevant tool for developing novel  targeted therapeutics  some of the unique aspects of this panel  include  pathways relevant only in specific  cancer types  that includes pathways like estrogen  signaling relevant in breast ovarian and  uterine cancer  androgen signaling and prostate cancer  and then the nrf2 pathway which has  become of key importance in lung cancers  we also covered a number of different  tumor antigens that are themselves the  focus of some targeted therapies  things like prostate specific antigen  secam  gm2 which of course uh is not a gene but  it's  the enzyme that produces it can be  targeted and then new since 16.  we also have a very comprehensive  breadth of coverage  for cancer relevant pathways this  includes everything from checkpoints  to metabolism to stemness and canonical  cancer pathways  and then we also added some novel  pathways at least for us  in our oncology panels pathways like nf  kappa b  immortality and stemness and the hippo  pathway  which is a novel pathway investigated by  many people for its  uh method of integrating both cell size  cell cycle and the tumor micro  environment  and then finally we included our 18 gene  tumor inflammation signature the  signature measures a peripherally  suppressed  adaptive immune response and i'll talk a  little bit more about the development  of that signature and its role in  in the data analysis for some of our  other 360 panels  in some coming slides  we also use some unique sourcing and  annotation for this panel  it's always important especially in  cancer where  pathway components can be mutated or  deleted  or otherwise mis-expressed to measure  the components of these important  pathways themselves  however it's also important to measure  genes that are transcriptionally  regulated by these pathways  in order to measure the up or down  regulation of pathway activity itself  and so to try to get it that we included  for consideration a number of signatures  these signatures are published in the  literature a few of them are listed  below  and these signatures have been again  published  to uh to to measure pathway activity  specifically  and thus were considered for inclusion  in in the panel  we also uh used the full tcga solid  tumor data set for most  pathways but with the exception of a key  few  these included the estrogen receptor  signaling panel which  estrogen receptor signaling of course  which is only important or only  has signal in breast and uterine cancers  and then further the androgen receptor  which focus we focused on prostate  cancer as most of the signal for that  pathway as well  is only going to be seen in prostate  cancer and we wanted to make sure  that we selected the genes that were  going to be most informative  in those specific cancers where they  have relevance  and then finally we spent quite a bit of  effort to produce  slimmer annotations for maximally  informative pathway analysis  as many of you know the nature of these  pathways is such  that many pathways traffic in and  through each other  during the course of signaling  especially pathways like map kinase  and pi3 kinase that act as sort of  