ncer 360 panels that will  give you  an idea of the kind of biology and  results that you should expect to get  from the tumor signaling 360 panel  so first i'll tell you the story about  how the tumor inflammation signature was  developed  it's not just a great story to  demonstrate the power of this signature  but also to demonstrate the  possibilities  of what kinds of signatures and  information you can get out of  encounter profiling this work was done  uh by merck in combination with their  efforts around their anti-pd-1 drug  pembrolizumab they started with ffpe  tumor tissue  taken from patients uh prior to  receiving the drug  rna was just extracted and analyzed on  the encounter platform  uh depending on the custom product they  were working with they got either  anywhere from uh 400 to 600 some genes  uh of information out of their and they  used that data  to try to discover genes and signatures  associated with anti-pd1 response  so when developing their signature they  started with that initial data set  and defined and validated an initial  signature  in melanoma they continued to validate  and refine that signature  and head neck gastric cancer and then  when they had an excellent candidate for  their gene expression profile  or gep they generated the final score  based on a penalized regression model  and nine different solid tumors  and then finally that gene exploration  profile validated  versus pdl-1ic and pdf1  unselected population  the final 18 gene tumor inflammation  signature was published  paper shown here is an excellent read if  any of you are interested  the two nations i mentioned previously  quantifies a peripherally suppressed  adaptive immune response one of the  things about the signal that's unique  and helpful is that it actually contains  very much  target genes as many of you know in  developing signatures you don't always  end up measuring genes  that look related to the biology you're  trying to measure  in this case and owing to the fact that  tumor inflammation signature was  developed from targeted panels  that were already focused on the biology  of interest merck was very  quickly able to get to an informative  and biologically meaningful signature  for response to pembrolizumab the  signature is a weighted linear sum of  genes  with a and that cut off uh is able to  determine whether a tumor is immune hot  or cold and predicts likelihood of  response to pembrolizumab and many other  checkpoint therapies  in addition to the tumor inflammation  signature pain cancer i o 360 was  designed with a number of  other signatures in mind many of these  are related to the immune response as  that's the primary focus of the panel  but a few of those are also related to  the tumor itself  and to the micro environment  the tumor signaling 360 panel has that  tumor inflammation signature  and all of the immune cell type  signatures that are shown on the right  um but the but overall um  none of these other signatures have been  covered albeit the biology  is very much still covered for many of  these  biological areas  so in our first case study this was an  io360 profiling experiment  on a multi-tumor cohort from the certum  institute in paris  there was was a heavily pre-treated  population of patients  from a variety of different tumor types  the initial results are shown here  all of the items on the right hand side  of the graph  are various cell types and signatures  from the io360 panel  and you can see that these signatures  and cell types  end up clustering the samples into three  primary groups  those that are immune cold immune  intermediate  or immune hot this is sort of a common  feature  of many studies where we can very easily  segregate the tumors using this data  into these into at least two or three  categories based on immune status  when we did a gene level analysis of the  data we found  that a number of genes included in the  tumor inflammation signature  were associated with objective response  here you can see stat1 ido1 and cxcl9  again all components of the tumor  inflammation signature  are associated with the objective  response  also in a signature level analysis a  number of different signatures  including tests were associated with  objective response  first you can see tumor inflammation  signal signature itself  highlighted in the bold box but then  there were also a number of other  predictors including nk cells and th1  cells  both of those signatures included in  tumor signaling 360  and then a few key immune relevant genes  like ido1  pdl1 and pde1 all of which again  are so are included in that tumor  signaling 360 panel as well  you can also see here that overall  survival and overall response rate is  significantly associated with the tumor  inflammation signature score  again this is something very commonly  seen  in our studies with tumor inflammation  signature and multiple checkpoint  blockade studies  and then one of the other helpful  features of the i o 360 panel  is a single sample analysis that's  possible through  um a specific fee for service data  analysis that we do with that panel  in this case you can see the tumor  inflammation signature  score for this particular single sample  is high  and thus we would have predicted  response to checkpoint blockade  but unfortunately this patient did not  respond however what you can see on the  right  is a number of different signatures for  this particular sample  plotted against the population of  samples in this study  and what we can see is that even though  pd1 expression  is relatively intermediate in this  particular sample  b7h3 expression is high and maybe  the cause or the explanation for why  this particular patient  albeit having a health immune hot tumor  failed to respond to checkpoint blockade  and so now we'll switch gears and talk  next about our breast cancer 360 panel  it's important first to understand that  quite a bit of the biology  included in the breast cancer 360 panel  is  offered by this pam 50 signature the pam  50 signature  has its goal to break these samples into  four main subtypes of breast cancer  these are luminal a  luminal b her2 enriched and basal like  each of these various subtypes has  associated biology with it  both pathway biology and then biology  that's predictive  of the kind of treatment that should be  employed for each subtype  and so in this study uh the the study  was called the finx  tnbc study it is published and we were  fortunate enough to get access to the  samples from this study  it was a multi-center randomized phase 3  trial in  triple negative breast cancer which as  many of you know is one of the most  aggressive forms of breast cancer  and patients were treated with a novel  chemotherapeutic agent  called kappa cytobean there were  111 samples from this cohort some of  which  in the tx plus cex cohort  these patients were treated with uh  standard of care  chemotherapy plus kappa cytobean and  then the t  plus cef cohort these patients were  treated just with standard of care  chemotherapeutic cocktail all of these  samples were run  with the bc 360 panel and an additional  panel plus option  that included biology related to kappa  cytobeans specifically  and since cavacitabine is related to  fluorouracil  they included quite a bit of that  biology as well  so in the initial results we saw just  like we saw in the previous study  that bc360 successfully clustered the  samples  into an immune hot and immune cold  cohorts and these cohorts as you can see  in the bottom more clustered based on  various signatures or otherwise known as  mena genes  um and these signatures were um  supplemented by some of our cell type  profiling  again the same cell type profiling and  the tumor signaling 360 panel again we  see these  two key biological groups both the  immune hot  enemy and cold or depleted and enriched  and the anti-tumor immune activity  is what clusters those various samples  we also saw that the tumors in the  immune depleted subset  uh were distributed across the various  uh  tnbc subgroups so addition in addition  to pam-50  that does the initial subtyping the  panel also includes additional  triple negative breast cancer subtypes  and we see that these subtypes do not  necessarily predict  immune hot or cold tumors which is  definitely of interest because we know  that  some types of breast cancer predict  quite a bit of the biology of those  tumors  however in our results we saw that there  were a number of key immune markers  associated  with regression free survival in these  patients  first ido1 pdl1 and pdl2  all genes in the tumor signaling 360  panel  were significantly associated with  regression free survival and the  patients treated with capocitabine  and we also some some additional markers  associated with  improved regression-free survival in  these patients  and that included the endothelial cell  signature and the mast cell signature  again the mast cell signature is present  in the tumor signaling 360 panel as well  and so in summary the tumor signaling  360 panel  was designed to look at the  comprehensive biology behind the tumor  the tumor micro environment and the  immune response focusing  on tumor biology and primed for studies  investigating targeted therapeutics our  other 360 panels have already  and as you have seen shown great promise  and uncovering mechanisms of resistance  to various targeted therapies and we  look forward to the kinds of studies and  data that will be produced  on our new tumor signaling 360 panel  with that i'll thank you for your  attention and take any questions  thank you very much dr piazza for a very  informative presentation  few questions for you also  which of the 360 series  panel that um nanostring has  would be best suited for um  the study of a mythological malignancy  great question are our 360 panels  generally think  about those three areas of biology